@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- ca-interpretation-time.l4
--
-- The Interpretation Act (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. I-21: its rules of TIME, as a module other encodings can reuse.
-- It imports only `prelude` and `daydate`.
-- Every quotation below is a `-- src:N | text` line copied by script from the text file (`requote.py`, `srcrefs.py`).
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE TAKES AS INPUT, AND NEVER COMPUTES
--   * Holidays.  A caller supplies a test `FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN` that says which days are holidays
--     where the thing is to be done.  Section 35(1) defines "holiday" partly by Sunday and partly by days that
--     other law, proclamations, provincial Acts and municipal resolutions fix; no calendar is written here.
--   * The day of assent, the day a regulation was registered, and whether a regulation is of an exempted class.
--   * The days Parliament sits (section 39(1)).
--
-- WHAT THE ACT DOES NOT SAY, AND THIS MODULE REFUSES
--   * the length of a "week" (the word does not occur in the text);
--   * anything on "days of rest", "recess" or a day on which an office is closed (the text names only "holiday");
--   * deemed service or notice (the text has no rule on it);
--   * a count that is not a whole number of at least one.
--
-- READINGS TAKEN ARE LISTED, WITH THE READINGS REJECTED AND THE TEXT THAT LICENSES EACH, IN NOTES.md section 3 (the fork register);
-- a comment below that says "fork F-..." names its entry there.

§ `Nouns of the time module`

-- A moment is a boundary between two days.
-- Section 6 speaks of "the expiration of the previous day" and of "the commencement of the following day"; each names the
-- same instant as the other when the day is the neighbour: the expiration of 2026-03-09 is the commencement of 2026-03-10.
DECLARE `A moment` IS ONE OF
    `the expiration of`   HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `the commencement of` HAS `the day` IS A DATE

-- A period of time is a run of whole days, both ends inside it.
DECLARE `A period` HAS
    `the first day` IS A DATE
    `the last day`  IS A DATE

-- Section 6 and section 5: what an enactment itself says about when it comes into force.
DECLARE `What an enactment says about when it comes into force` IS ONE OF
    `it names no day`
    `it names a particular day`
        HAS `the day named` IS A DATE
    `it says the day is to be fixed later`
        HAS `the day when fixed` IS A MAYBE DATE

-- The answer: a moment, or the named state that no day yet decides it.
DECLARE `Coming into force` IS ONE OF
    `in force from`
        HAS `the moment` IS A `A moment`
    `there is as yet no day from which it binds`

-- Section 27(3), (4) and (5): the word that ties a time to a specified day decides whether that day is in the time.
DECLARE `A word tying the start of a time to a day` IS ONE OF
    `begins at`
    `begins on`
    `begins with`
    `begins after`
    `is from`

DECLARE `A word tying the end of a time to a day` IS ONE OF
    `ends at`
    `ends on`
    `ends with`
    `continues to`
    `continues until`
    `is done before`

-- Section 37(1): the kinds of "year" the Act distinguishes.
DECLARE `A year as the Act reads it` IS ONE OF
    `a calendar year`
    `a financial year or fiscal year`
    `a Dominical year`

§ `Named refusals: what the Act does not answer`

-- A refusal is an expression at any type, so one definition serves every function that reaches the gap.
-- src:227 | 28 Where there is a reference to a period of time consisting of a number of months after or before a specified day, the period is calculated by
@ref Interpretation Act s 28 (src:227)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Act has no rule for a count that is not a whole number of at least one` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Act has no rule for a count that is not a whole number of at least one"

-- The text has no occurrence of the word "week".  A week is therefore not defined, and neither is how weeks are counted.
@ref Interpretation Act: no provision; the word "week" does not occur in the text
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Act does not define a week or say how a period of weeks is counted` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Act does not define a week or say how a period of weeks is counted"

-- The only day-off word in the text is "holiday" (section 35(1)).
@ref Interpretation Act: no provision; "day of rest", "recess" and "closed" do not occur in the text
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Act has no rule for a day of rest, a recess or a day on which an office is closed` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Act has no rule for a day of rest, a recess or a day on which an office is closed, other than a holiday"

-- The text has no rule on service or notice.
@ref Interpretation Act: no provision; nothing in the text deems a document served or a notice given
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Act has no rule on deemed service or notice` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Act has no rule on deemed service or notice"

-- Not a refusal in the text: a calendar the caller must supply.
-- src:215 | 26 Where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls on a holiday, the thing may be done on the day next following that is not a holiday.
@ref Interpretation Act s 26 (src:215)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`every day tried was a holiday by the calendar supplied` MEANS
    REFUSE "every one of the days tried after the last day was a holiday by the calendar supplied; the bound of 366 days is this encoding's, not the Act's"

§ `Section 2(1): "regulation"`

-- A regulation is any instrument issued, made or established in the execution of a power conferred by or under the authority of an Act,
-- or by or under the authority of the Governor in Council.  The listed kinds (order, rule, by-law and so on) add nothing:
-- the definition says "includes", and "other instrument" closes the list, so the kind does not matter, the two limbs do.
-- src:20 | regulation includes an order, regulation, rule, rule of court, form, tariff of costs or fees, letters patent, commission, warrant, proclamation, by-law, resolution or other instrument issued, made or established
-- src:21 | (a) in the execution of a power conferred by or under the authority of an Act, or
-- src:22 | (b) by or under the authority of the Governor in Council; (règlement)
@ref Interpretation Act s 2(1) "regulation" (src:20-22)
GIVEN `in the execution of a power conferred by or under the authority of an Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `by or under the authority of the Governor in Council` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is a regulation` `in the execution of a power conferred by or under the authority of an Act` `by or under the authority of the Governor in Council` MEANS
    IF      `in the execution of a power conferred by or under the authority of an Act`
         OR `by or under the authority of the Governor in Council`
    THEN TRUE
    ELSE `the definition of regulation says what it includes, and says nothing of an instrument issued under neither limb`

-- src:20 | regulation includes an order, regulation, rule, rule of court, form, tariff of costs or fees, letters patent, commission, warrant, proclamation, by-law, resolution or other instrument issued, made or established
@ref Interpretation Act s 2(1) "regulation" (src:20)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the definition of regulation says what it includes, and says nothing of an instrument issued under neither limb` MEANS
    REFUSE "the definition of regulation says what it includes, and says nothing of an instrument issued under neither limb"

§ `Section 28: a period of months after or before a specified day`

-- Section 28 is three steps, written as three functions so that a reviewer can hold the section beside them.
-- Months are numbered from the year 0: the month index is 12 times the year, plus the month less one.

-- (a) count the months, without including the month in which the specified day falls.
-- src:228 | (a) counting forward or backward from the specified day the number of months, without including the month in which that day falls;
@ref Interpretation Act s 28(a) (src:228)
GIVEN `a day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the month index of` `a day` MEANS
    (DATE_YEAR `a day` TIMES 12) PLUS (DATE_MONTH `a day` MINUS 1)

-- src:228 | (a) counting forward or backward from the specified day the number of months, without including the month in which that day falls;
@ref Interpretation Act s 28(a) (src:228)
GIVEN `a count` IS A NUMBER
      `the specified day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the month reached counting` `a count` `months forward from` `the specified day` MEANS
    `the month index of` `the specified day` PLUS `a count`

-- src:228 | (a) counting forward or backward from the specified day the number of months, without including the month in which that day falls;
@ref Interpretation Act s 28(a) (src:228)
GIVEN `a count` IS A NUMBER
      `the specified day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the month reached counting` `a count` `months backward from` `the specified day` MEANS
    `the month index of` `the specified day` MINUS `a count`

-- (c) include, in the last month counted, the day that has the same calendar number as the specified day or,
--     if that month has no day with that number, the last day of that month.
-- src:230 | (c) including in the last month counted under paragraph (a) the day that has the same calendar number as the specified day or, if that month has no day with that number, the last day of that month.
@ref Interpretation Act s 28(c) (src:230)
GIVEN `a month index` IS A NUMBER
      `the specified day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day counted in the month with index` `a month index` `for a period counted from` `the specified day` MEANS
    YMD targetYear targetMonth numberCounted
    WHERE
        targetYear    MEANS FLOOR (`a month index` DIVIDED BY 12)
        targetMonth   MEANS (`a month index` MODULO 12) PLUS 1
        lastNumber    MEANS `Days in month` targetMonth targetYear
        numberCounted MEANS IF   DATE_DAY `the specified day` AT MOST lastNumber
                            THEN DATE_DAY `the specified day`
                            ELSE lastNumber

-- A whole number of at least one.  The text says "a number of months"; a half month, a zero or a negative count is not a
-- count the section tells you how to make.
GIVEN `a count` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a count of whole units, at least one` `a count` MEANS
        `a count` AT LEAST 1
    AND FLOOR `a count` EQUALS `a count`

-- (b) exclude the specified day: the first day of a period after it is the next day.
-- src:227 | 28 Where there is a reference to a period of time consisting of a number of months after or before a specified day, the period is calculated by
-- src:228 | (a) counting forward or backward from the specified day the number of months, without including the month in which that day falls;
-- src:229 | (b) excluding the specified day; and
-- src:230 | (c) including in the last month counted under paragraph (a) the day that has the same calendar number as the specified day or, if that month has no day with that number, the last day of that month.
@ref Interpretation Act s 28 (src:227-230)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `months after` d MEANS
    IF   `a count of whole units, at least one` n
    THEN `A period`
             WITH `the first day` IS d PLUS 1
                  `the last day`  IS `the day counted in the month with index` (`the month reached counting` n `months forward from` d) `for a period counted from` d
    ELSE `the Act has no rule for a count that is not a whole number of at least one`

-- Section 28 says "after or before"; the brief's cases say "from".  Section 27(4) and (5) treat "after" and "from" alike
-- for a day, so a period of months "from" a day is read as a period of months "after" it (fork F-FROM).
-- src:227 | 28 Where there is a reference to a period of time consisting of a number of months after or before a specified day, the period is calculated by
-- src:228 | (a) counting forward or backward from the specified day the number of months, without including the month in which that day falls;
-- src:229 | (b) excluding the specified day; and
-- src:230 | (c) including in the last month counted under paragraph (a) the day that has the same calendar number as the specified day or, if that month has no day with that number, the last day of that month.
@ref Interpretation Act s 28 (src:227-230)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `months from` d MEANS
    `the period of` n `months after` d

-- Counting backward, the last month counted is the earliest, so the day included in it is the FIRST day of the period,
-- and the specified day (excluded by (b)) is the day after its last.
-- src:227 | 28 Where there is a reference to a period of time consisting of a number of months after or before a specified day, the period is calculated by
-- src:228 | (a) counting forward or backward from the specified day the number of months, without including the month in which that day falls;
-- src:229 | (b) excluding the specified day; and
-- src:230 | (c) including in the last month counted under paragraph (a) the day that has the same calendar number as the specified day or, if that month has no day with that number, the last day of that month.
@ref Interpretation Act s 28 (src:227-230)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `months before` d MEANS
    IF   `a count of whole units, at least one` n
    THEN `A period`
             WITH `the first day` IS `the day counted in the month with index` (`the month reached counting` n `months backward from` d) `for a period counted from` d
                  `the last day`  IS d MINUS 1
    ELSE `the Act has no rule for a count that is not a whole number of at least one`

§ `Section 37(1): "year" is twelve consecutive months`

-- src:366 | 37 (1) The expression year means any period of twelve consecutive months, except that a reference
@ref Interpretation Act s 37(1) (src:366)
`months in a year as the Act reads it` MEANS 12

-- Section 28 speaks of months only.  Section 37(1) says a year is "any period of twelve consecutive months", so a period of
-- N years after a day is a period of 12N months after it, and section 28 counts it (fork F-YEAR).
-- src:366 | 37 (1) The expression year means any period of twelve consecutive months, except that a reference
@ref Interpretation Act s 37(1) (src:366)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `years after` d MEANS
    IF   `a count of whole units, at least one` n
    THEN `the period of` (n TIMES `months in a year as the Act reads it`) `months after` d
    ELSE `the Act has no rule for a count that is not a whole number of at least one`

-- src:366 | 37 (1) The expression year means any period of twelve consecutive months, except that a reference
@ref Interpretation Act s 37(1) (src:366)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `years from` d MEANS
    `the period of` n `years after` d

-- src:366 | 37 (1) The expression year means any period of twelve consecutive months, except that a reference
@ref Interpretation Act s 37(1) (src:366)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `years before` d MEANS
    IF   `a count of whole units, at least one` n
    THEN `the period of` (n TIMES `months in a year as the Act reads it`) `months before` d
    ELSE `the Act has no rule for a count that is not a whole number of at least one`

§ `Section 27: days, clear days, and the words that tie a time to a day`

-- "From" and "after" a specified day: the day is not counted (s 27(4)); the same where a thing is to be done "within a time
-- after, from, of or before" it (s 27(5)).  A period of N days after d therefore runs from d + 1 to d + N, and the day d + N is its last
-- day.  The text says the time does not include the specified day; it does not say in so many words that the Nth day after it is
-- inside, which is the arithmetic consequence of counting N days that begin the day after (fork F-LASTDAY).
-- src:223 | (4) Where a time is expressed to begin after or to be from a specified day, the time does not include that day.
-- src:225 | (5) Where anything is to be done within a time after, from, of or before a specified day, the time does not include that day.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(4) (src:223) and s 27(5) (src:225)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `days after` d MEANS
    IF   `a count of whole units, at least one` n
    THEN `A period`
             WITH `the first day` IS d PLUS 1
                  `the last day`  IS d PLUS n
    ELSE `the Act has no rule for a count that is not a whole number of at least one`

-- src:223 | (4) Where a time is expressed to begin after or to be from a specified day, the time does not include that day.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(4) (src:223)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `days from` d MEANS
    `the period of` n `days after` d

-- "Before" a specified day: the day is not counted either (s 27(5)), so N days before d run from d - N to d - 1.
-- src:225 | (5) Where anything is to be done within a time after, from, of or before a specified day, the time does not include that day.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(5) (src:225)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `days before` d MEANS
    IF   `a count of whole units, at least one` n
    THEN `A period`
             WITH `the first day` IS d MINUS n
                  `the last day`  IS d MINUS 1
    ELSE `the Act has no rule for a count that is not a whole number of at least one`

-- Weeks: the Act has no provision.  These three never answer.
@ref Interpretation Act: no provision; the word "week" does not occur in the text
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `weeks after` d MEANS
    `the Act does not define a week or say how a period of weeks is counted`

@ref Interpretation Act: no provision; the word "week" does not occur in the text
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `weeks from` d MEANS
    `the Act does not define a week or say how a period of weeks is counted`

@ref Interpretation Act: no provision; the word "week" does not occur in the text
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the period of` n `weeks before` d MEANS
    `the Act does not define a week or say how a period of weeks is counted`

-- A time bounded by two specified days, each tied to its day by a word of the text.
-- (3): a time that begins or ends "at, on or with" a day, or continues "to or until" it, includes that day.
-- (4): a time that begins "after" a day, or is "from" it, does not include that day.
-- (5): a thing to be done "within a time ... before" a day: the time does not include that day.
-- src:221 | (3) Where a time is expressed to begin or end at, on or with a specified day, or to continue to or until a specified day, the time includes that day.
-- src:223 | (4) Where a time is expressed to begin after or to be from a specified day, the time does not include that day.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(3) (src:221) and s 27(4) (src:223)
GIVEN `the start word` IS A `A word tying the start of a time to a day`
      `the start day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day of a time bounded at its start by` `the start word` `the start day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the start word`
    WHEN `begins at`    THEN `the start day`
    WHEN `begins on`    THEN `the start day`
    WHEN `begins with`  THEN `the start day`
    WHEN `begins after` THEN `the start day` PLUS 1
    WHEN `is from`      THEN `the start day` PLUS 1

-- src:221 | (3) Where a time is expressed to begin or end at, on or with a specified day, or to continue to or until a specified day, the time includes that day.
-- src:225 | (5) Where anything is to be done within a time after, from, of or before a specified day, the time does not include that day.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(3) (src:221) and s 27(5) (src:225)
GIVEN `the end word` IS A `A word tying the end of a time to a day`
      `the end day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a time bounded at its end by` `the end word` `the end day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the end word`
    WHEN `ends at`         THEN `the end day`
    WHEN `ends on`         THEN `the end day`
    WHEN `ends with`       THEN `the end day`
    WHEN `continues to`    THEN `the end day`
    WHEN `continues until` THEN `the end day`
    WHEN `is done before`  THEN `the end day` MINUS 1

-- src:221 | (3) Where a time is expressed to begin or end at, on or with a specified day, or to continue to or until a specified day, the time includes that day.
-- src:223 | (4) Where a time is expressed to begin after or to be from a specified day, the time does not include that day.
-- src:225 | (5) Where anything is to be done within a time after, from, of or before a specified day, the time does not include that day.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(3) (src:221), s 27(4) (src:223) and s 27(5) (src:225)
GIVEN `the start word` IS A `A word tying the start of a time to a day`
      `the start day` IS A DATE
      `the end word` IS A `A word tying the end of a time to a day`
      `the end day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A period`
`the time bounded by` `the start word` `the start day` `the end word` `the end day` MEANS
    `A period`
        WITH `the first day` IS `the first day of a time bounded at its start by` `the start word` `the start day`
             `the last day`  IS `the last day of a time bounded at its end by` `the end word` `the end day`

-- How many days a period holds: both ends are inside it.
GIVEN p IS A `A period`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of days in` p MEANS
    (Day (p's `the last day`) MINUS Day (p's `the first day`)) PLUS 1

-- Whether a day falls inside a period: the first day and the last day are inside, the day before the first and the day
-- after the last are outside.
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      p IS A `A period`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
d `falls inside` p MEANS
        p's `the first day` AT MOST d
    AND d AT MOST p's `the last day`

-- Two events.  Section 27(1): for "clear days", and for "at least" a number of days between two events, the days on which
-- the events happen are both excluded.  Section 27(2): for a number of days not expressed to be clear days, the day of the first
-- event is excluded and the day of the second is included.
-- The text counts between two events and says nothing about two events on one day or in the wrong order: those refuse.
-- src:217 | 27 (1) Where there is a reference to a number of clear days or “at least” a number of days between two events, in calculating that number of days the days on which the events happen are excluded.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(1) (src:217)
GIVEN a IS A DATE
      b IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of clear days between` a `and` b MEANS
    IF   b GREATER THAN a
    THEN (Day b MINUS Day a) MINUS 1
    ELSE `the Act counts days between two events only where the second event is on a later day than the first`

-- src:219 | (2) Where there is a reference to a number of days, not expressed to be clear days, between two events, in calculating that number of days the day on which the first event happens is excluded and the day on which the second event happens is included.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(2) (src:219)
GIVEN a IS A DATE
      b IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of days, not expressed to be clear days, between` a `and` b MEANS
    IF   b GREATER THAN a
    THEN Day b MINUS Day a
    ELSE `the Act counts days between two events only where the second event is on a later day than the first`

-- src:217 | 27 (1) Where there is a reference to a number of clear days or “at least” a number of days between two events, in calculating that number of days the days on which the events happen are excluded.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(1) (src:217)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      a IS A DATE
      b IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`there are at least` n `clear days between` a `and` b MEANS
    `the number of clear days between` a `and` b AT LEAST n

-- The earliest day for the second event, if at least n clear days must lie between it and the first event on day a:
-- the n days after a are clear, and the second event falls on the day after them.
-- src:217 | 27 (1) Where there is a reference to a number of clear days or “at least” a number of days between two events, in calculating that number of days the days on which the events happen are excluded.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(1) (src:217)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      a IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the earliest day of the second event, if at least` n `clear days must lie between it and the first event on` a MEANS
    a PLUS (n PLUS 1)

-- src:219 | (2) Where there is a reference to a number of days, not expressed to be clear days, between two events, in calculating that number of days the day on which the first event happens is excluded and the day on which the second event happens is included.
@ref Interpretation Act s 27(2) (src:219)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      a IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day of the second event, if` n `days not expressed to be clear days lie between it and the first event on` a MEANS
    a PLUS n

@ref Interpretation Act, no provision: the text counts days between events only for events on days
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Act counts days between two events only where the second event is on a later day than the first` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Act counts days between two events only where the second event is on a later day than the first"

§ `Section 26: a time limit that ends on a holiday`

-- The holiday test is the caller's.  Section 35(1) defines "holiday" by Sunday, by named days, by days fixed by proclamation,
-- by provincial Acts and by municipal resolutions; where the thing is to be done decides which of those count.
-- "The day next following that is not a holiday": the first later day that the test does not mark, however many holidays
-- run in a row.  The search stops after 366 days and then refuses; the bound is this encoding's, not the Act's.
-- src:215 | 26 Where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls on a holiday, the thing may be done on the day next following that is not a holiday.
@ref Interpretation Act s 26 (src:215)
GIVEN `a holiday` IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
      `a day` IS A DATE
      `the number of days still to try` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day next following, that is not a holiday` `a holiday` `a day` `the number of days still to try` MEANS
    IF   `the number of days still to try` AT MOST 0
    THEN `every day tried was a holiday by the calendar supplied`
    ELSE IF   `a holiday` nextDay
         THEN `the day next following, that is not a holiday` `a holiday` nextDay (`the number of days still to try` MINUS 1)
         ELSE nextDay
    WHERE
        nextDay MEANS `a day` PLUS 1

-- Where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls on a holiday, the thing may be done on the day next
-- following that is not a holiday.  Otherwise the day is the day.
-- Section 26 names the time "limited for the doing of a thing" and says nothing of its unit, so it reaches a limit counted in
-- months or years as well as in days (fork F-26-UNITS).  It moves the day on which the THING may be done; it does not move the
-- period, and the functions below keep the two apart.
-- src:215 | 26 Where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls on a holiday, the thing may be done on the day next following that is not a holiday.
@ref Interpretation Act s 26 (src:215)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      h IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day on which a thing may be done when the time limited ends on` d `given the holidays` h MEANS
    IF   h d
    THEN `the day next following, that is not a holiday` h d 366
    ELSE d

-- Whether the thing may be done on a day, within a period, where the last day of the period may be a holiday.
-- Literal reading: the days inside the period, and the one later day that section 26 names when the last day is a holiday.
-- A holiday that lies between those two days is not named by the section and is not added (fork F-26-GAP).
-- src:215 | 26 Where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls on a holiday, the thing may be done on the day next following that is not a holiday.
@ref Interpretation Act s 26 (src:215)
GIVEN e IS A DATE
      p IS A `A period`
      h IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a thing may be done on` e `within the time limited by` p `given the holidays` h MEANS
        e `falls inside` p
    OR (    h (p's `the last day`)
        AND e EQUALS `the last day on which a thing may be done when the time limited ends on` (p's `the last day`) `given the holidays` h)

-- The brief's six counted periods, then section 26 on each: the last day on which a thing may be done.
-- src:215 | 26 Where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls on a holiday, the thing may be done on the day next following that is not a holiday.
@ref Interpretation Act ss 27(4), 27(5) and 26 (src:215)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      h IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day on which a thing may be done within` n `days after` d `given the holidays` h MEANS
    `the last day on which a thing may be done when the time limited ends on` ((`the period of` n `days after` d)'s `the last day`) `given the holidays` h

-- src:215 | 26 Where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls on a holiday, the thing may be done on the day next following that is not a holiday.
@ref Interpretation Act ss 28 and 26 (src:215)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      h IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day on which a thing may be done within` n `months after` d `given the holidays` h MEANS
    `the last day on which a thing may be done when the time limited ends on` ((`the period of` n `months after` d)'s `the last day`) `given the holidays` h

-- src:215 | 26 Where the time limited for the doing of a thing expires or falls on a holiday, the thing may be done on the day next following that is not a holiday.
@ref Interpretation Act ss 37(1), 28 and 26 (src:215)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      h IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day on which a thing may be done within` n `years after` d `given the holidays` h MEANS
    `the last day on which a thing may be done when the time limited ends on` ((`the period of` n `years after` d)'s `the last day`) `given the holidays` h

-- The Act speaks of a holiday and of nothing else that closes a day.
-- A caller who has a day of rest, a recess or a closed office that is NOT a holiday gets this refusal, not a guess.
@ref Interpretation Act: no provision for a day of rest, a recess or a closed office, other than "holiday" in s 26
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day for a thing where the time limited ends on a day of rest, a recess or a closed office that is not a holiday` d MEANS
    `the Act has no rule for a day of rest, a recess or a day on which an office is closed`

§ `Moments: the boundary between two days`

-- The first day that starts at or after a moment.
-- Section 6(1) speaks of coming into force "on the expiration of the previous day" and of ceasing to have effect "on the
-- commencement of the following day": one instant each, on the line between two days.  An enactment in force from the
-- expiration of D is in force on D + 1 and every later day, and not on D.
GIVEN m IS A `A moment`
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day that starts at or after` m MEANS
    CONSIDER m
    WHEN `the expiration of` d   THEN d PLUS 1
    WHEN `the commencement of` d THEN d

-- The expiration of 2026-03-09 and the commencement of 2026-03-10 are the same instant.
GIVEN m IS A `A moment`
      n IS A `A moment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`two moments are the same instant` m `and` n MEANS
    `the first day that starts at or after` m EQUALS `the first day that starts at or after` n

-- An enactment in force from a moment is in force on a day if the day starts at or after that moment.
GIVEN m IS A `A moment`
      e IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an enactment in force from` m `is in force on` e MEANS
    e AT LEAST `the first day that starts at or after` m

-- An enactment that ceases to have effect at a moment is still in force on a day if the day starts before that moment.
GIVEN m IS A `A moment`
      e IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an enactment that ceases to have effect at` m `is still in force on` e MEANS
    e LESS THAN `the first day that starts at or after` m

-- The day an enactment that has begun to bind binds from; the named state has no day.
-- src:48 | (2) If no date of commencement is provided for in an Act, the date of commencement of that Act is the date of assent to the Act.
-- src:56 | 6 (1) Where an enactment is expressed to come into force on a particular day, it shall be construed as coming into force on the expiration of the previous day, and where an enactment is expressed to expire, lapse or otherwise cease to have effect on a particular day, it shall be construed as ceasing to have effect on the commencement of the following day.
@ref Interpretation Act s 5(2) (src:48) and s 6(1) (src:56)
GIVEN c IS A `Coming into force`
GIVETH A DATE
`the day from which it binds` c MEANS
    CONSIDER c
    WHEN `in force from` m THEN `the first day that starts at or after` m
    WHEN `there is as yet no day from which it binds` THEN `no day yet decides the coming into force`

@ref Interpretation Act: no provision; until a day is fixed or a regulation is registered the text gives no day
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`no day yet decides the coming into force` MEANS
    REFUSE "no day yet decides the coming into force: the day is still to be fixed, or the regulation has not been registered"

§ `Sections 5 and 6: when an Act comes into force`

-- What fixes an Act's commencement: the day it was assented to (the Clerk of the Parliaments endorses it, s 5(1)), and what the
-- provision in question says about its own coming into force.  A "provision" is the whole Act or any portion of it, because an
-- enactment is an Act or regulation "or any portion" of one (s 2(1)).
DECLARE `Commencement facts of an Act` HAS
    `the day the Act was assented to` IS A DATE
    `what the provision says about when it comes into force` IS A `What an enactment says about when it comes into force`

-- s 5(2): if no date of commencement is provided for, the date of commencement is the date of assent.
-- src:48 | (2) If no date of commencement is provided for in an Act, the date of commencement of that Act is the date of assent to the Act.
@ref Interpretation Act s 5(2) (src:48)
GIVEN `the day the Act was assented to` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the date of commencement of an Act that provides none, assented to on` `the day the Act was assented to` MEANS
    `the day the Act was assented to`

-- The day and the moment.
-- s 5(2) gives the date (the date of assent).  s 6(2)(a) gives the moment: "on the expiration of the day immediately before the
-- day the Act was assented to".  s 5(4) carries the same answer to the remaining provisions when other provisions are given other
-- days.  s 6(1) gives the moment for a provision that names a particular day: the expiration of the day before it.
-- A day "to be fixed later" is read as a particular day once it is fixed, and until then no day decides it (fork F-FIXED-LATER).
-- src:48 | (2) If no date of commencement is provided for in an Act, the date of commencement of that Act is the date of assent to the Act.
-- src:52 | (4) Where an Act provides that certain provisions thereof are to come or are deemed to have come into force on a day other than the date of assent to the Act, the remaining provisions of the Act are deemed to have come into force on the date of assent to the Act.
-- src:56 | 6 (1) Where an enactment is expressed to come into force on a particular day, it shall be construed as coming into force on the expiration of the previous day, and where an enactment is expressed to expire, lapse or otherwise cease to have effect on a particular day, it shall be construed as ceasing to have effect on the commencement of the following day.
-- src:58 | (2) Every enactment that is not expressed to come into force on a particular day shall be construed as coming into force
-- src:59 | (a) in the case of an Act, on the expiration of the day immediately before the day the Act was assented to in Her Majesty’s name; and
@ref Interpretation Act s 5(2) (src:48), s 5(4) (src:52), s 6(1) (src:56) and s 6(2)(a) (src:58-59)
GIVEN `the facts` IS A `Commencement facts of an Act`
GIVETH A `Coming into force`
`the coming into force of the provision of an Act` `the facts` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the facts`'s `what the provision says about when it comes into force`
    WHEN `it names no day` THEN
        `in force from` (`the expiration of` (`the facts`'s `the day the Act was assented to` MINUS 1))
    WHEN `it names a particular day` named THEN
        `in force from` (`the expiration of` (named MINUS 1))
    WHEN `it says the day is to be fixed later` fixed THEN
        CONSIDER fixed
        WHEN NOTHING THEN `there is as yet no day from which it binds`
        WHEN JUST fixedDay THEN `in force from` (`the expiration of` (fixedDay MINUS 1))

-- s 5(3): where an Act contains a provision that the Act or a portion is to come into force on a day later than the date of assent,
-- that provision is deemed to have come into force on the date of assent.  It is a provision that names no day for itself,
-- so s 6(2)(a) gives its moment.
-- src:50 | (3) Where an Act contains a provision that the Act or any portion thereof is to come into force on a day later than the date of assent to the Act, that provision is deemed to have come into force on the date of assent to the Act.
-- src:58 | (2) Every enactment that is not expressed to come into force on a particular day shall be construed as coming into force
-- src:59 | (a) in the case of an Act, on the expiration of the day immediately before the day the Act was assented to in Her Majesty’s name; and
@ref Interpretation Act s 5(3) (src:50) and s 6(2)(a) (src:58-59)
GIVEN `the day the Act was assented to` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `A moment`
`the moment from which a provision postponing commencement is itself in force, for an Act assented to on` `the day the Act was assented to` MEANS
    `the expiration of` (`the day the Act was assented to` MINUS 1)

§ `Section 6(2)(b) and 6(3): when a regulation comes into force`

-- What fixes a regulation's commencement.  Section 6 of the Statutory Instruments Act and subsection 5(1) of that Act are
-- not in the text, so the registration day, and whether the regulation is of an exempted class, are inputs.
DECLARE `Commencement facts of a regulation` HAS
    `the day the regulation was made` IS A DATE
    `what the regulation says about when it comes into force` IS A `What an enactment says about when it comes into force`
    `the day it was registered under section 6 of the Statutory Instruments Act, if it has been` IS A MAYBE DATE
    `it is of a class exempted from the application of subsection 5(1) of that Act` IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:56 | 6 (1) Where an enactment is expressed to come into force on a particular day, it shall be construed as coming into force on the expiration of the previous day, and where an enactment is expressed to expire, lapse or otherwise cease to have effect on a particular day, it shall be construed as ceasing to have effect on the commencement of the following day.
-- src:58 | (2) Every enactment that is not expressed to come into force on a particular day shall be construed as coming into force
-- src:59 | (a) in the case of an Act, on the expiration of the day immediately before the day the Act was assented to in Her Majesty’s name; and
-- src:60 | (b) in the case of a regulation, on the expiration of the day immediately before the day the regulation was registered pursuant to section 6 of the Statutory Instruments Act or, if the regulation is of a class that is exempted from the application of subsection 5(1) of that Act, on the expiration of the day immediately before the day the regulation was made.
@ref Interpretation Act s 6(1) (src:56) and s 6(2)(b) (src:58-60)
GIVEN `the facts` IS A `Commencement facts of a regulation`
GIVETH A `Coming into force`
`the coming into force of a regulation` `the facts` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the facts`'s `what the regulation says about when it comes into force`
    WHEN `it names a particular day` named THEN
        `in force from` (`the expiration of` (named MINUS 1))
    WHEN `it says the day is to be fixed later` fixed THEN
        CONSIDER fixed
        WHEN NOTHING THEN `there is as yet no day from which it binds`
        WHEN JUST fixedDay THEN `in force from` (`the expiration of` (fixedDay MINUS 1))
    WHEN `it names no day` THEN
        IF   `the facts`'s `it is of a class exempted from the application of subsection 5(1) of that Act`
        THEN `in force from` (`the expiration of` (`the facts`'s `the day the regulation was made` MINUS 1))
        ELSE CONSIDER `the facts`'s `the day it was registered under section 6 of the Statutory Instruments Act, if it has been`
             WHEN NOTHING THEN `there is as yet no day from which it binds`
             WHEN JUST registered THEN `in force from` (`the expiration of` (registered MINUS 1))

